# Femur Segmentation in Knee X-ray Images (U-Net)

Binary segmentation of the femur in knee X-ray images with a U-Net, written in PyTorch.
Course project (CNNs): 150 X-rays, of which 100 were manually annotated and 50 are an
unlabeled test set.

## Project structure

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `args.py` | Command-line arguments: directories, epochs, learning rate, model size, fine-tuning options |
| `dataset.py` | Image/mask reading function and the `FemurDataset` class (with light augmentation) |
| `model.py` | U-Net definition (Aleksei Tiulpin's U-Net, provided in the course) |
| `utils.py` | Dice loss and Dice score (provided in the course) |
| `trainer.py` | Model initialization, training loop, validation loop, checkpointing, training curves |
| `main.py` | Builds the data loaders and starts training |
| `evaluate.py` | Final evaluation: Dice score, predicted masks and example visualizations |

## Data

The data is not included in this repository. Expected layout (the folder passed as `--data_dir`):

```
<data_dir>/
 ├── xrays/    X-ray images (png / jpg)
 ├── masks/    binary masks, values 0 = background, 1 = femur
 └── CSVs/     dataset.csv, train.csv, val.csv, test.csv
```

Each CSV has two columns, `xrays` and `masks`, with paths relative to `<data_dir>`.
The 50 test images have `None` in the `masks` column.

- `dataset.csv`: all 150 images
- `train.csv` / `val.csv`: random 80 / 20 split of the 100 labeled images (seed 42)
- `test.csv`: the 50 unlabeled images

Masks made in Photopea (values 0/255) were converted to 0/1 so that all masks match the
Roboflow-labeled ones.

## Setup

```
pip install torch pillow numpy pandas matplotlib
```

For a GPU, install the CUDA build of PyTorch from https://pytorch.org (the default pip
build can be CPU-only on Windows). Check with
`python -c "import torch; print(torch.cuda.is_available())"`.

## Usage

Train (saves the best model by validation Dice to `outputs/best_model.pt`):

```
python main.py --data_dir "path/to/data" --output_dir outputs --epochs 50 --batch_size 8
```

Evaluate (Dice on train/val, predicted masks and plots for all splits):

```
python evaluate.py --data_dir "path/to/data" --output_dir outputs
```

Useful options (see `args.py` for all of them):

| Option | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `--img_size` | 256 | images and masks are resized to this square size |
| `--bw` / `--depth` | 24 / 6 | U-Net basic width and depth |
| `--lr` | 1e-3 | learning rate (Adam) |
| `--device` | auto | `auto`, `cpu` or `cuda` |
| `--checkpoint` | none | start from saved weights (fine-tuning) or evaluate another model |
| `--freeze_encoder` | 0 | freeze the encoder when fine-tuning |

## Method

- **Model:** U-Net, 1 input channel, 1 output channel (logits), width 24, depth 6, batch norm.
- **Loss:** binary cross-entropy + Dice loss (`dice_loss_from_logits` in `utils.py`).
- **Metric:** Dice score at threshold 0.5 (`dice_score_from_logits`).
- **Optimizer:** Adam, lr 1e-3, reduced on plateau of the validation Dice.
- **Augmentation (train only):** horizontal flip, random shift up to 10 %, brightness/contrast jitter.
- **Model selection:** the epoch with the best validation Dice is kept.

## Results

50 epochs, 256 x 256 input, batch size 8.

| Split | Images | Mean Dice |
|---|---|---|
| Train | 80 | 0.985 |
| Validation | 20 | 0.985 (std 0.005) |
| Test | 50 | not computable: no ground-truth masks |

Training curves are in `outputs/training_curves.png` and example predictions in
`outputs/examples_val.png` and `outputs/examples_test.png`.

## Credits

- U-Net implementation: Aleksei Tiulpin, University of Oulu (https://github.com/lext/deep-pipeline).
  The `Encoder`, `Decoder` and `conv_block_3x3` helpers in `model.py` are re-implementations
  matching the way his `UNet` class uses them.
- `utils.py` (Dice loss and score) provided by the course instructor.